{"@context":["https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/ns/content/v1.jsonld",{"@base":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio/"}],"@id":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/rem-sign-as-spin-structure","@type":["folio-assistant-core:Remark","doco:Section"],"label":"rem:sign-as-spin-structure","kind":"remark","title":"The sign of $\\sqrt{q}$ as a spin structure","uses":["papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/rem-sqrt-q-ribbon-structure","papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/rem-braiding-unit-circle-convention"],"interprets":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/rem-sqrt-q-ribbon-structure","tags":["ribbon","twist","sqrt-q","spin-structure","framing","post-archimedean"],"text":"sign-as-spin-structure.md","provenance":"authored","html":"<p><a id=\"rem:sign-as-spin-structure\"></a>\n<strong>Remark.</strong> <em>The sign of <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\sqrt{q}</code> as a spin structure</em></p>\n<p>The relation <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">v^2 = q</code> specifies <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">v = q^{1/2}</code> up to sign. Post-archimedean, this binary sign determines the spin structure on the realized worldline.</p>\n"}
